---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from "../../lib/seo";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  { q: "What is a BMR calculator?", a: "A BMR calculator estimates how many calories your body burns at complete rest for basic functions. It uses Mifflin-St Jeor equation with your age, sex, height, and weight." },
  { q: "What is my BMR?", a: "Your BMR varies based on your stats. Use our <a href=\"/bmr-calculator/\">BMR calculator</a> to get your exact number. A typical range: 1,200–2,000 calories/day depending on size and sex." },
  { q: "How is BMR calculated?", a: "Using Mifflin-St Jeor: Men BMR=10×weight+6.25×height-5×age+5; Women BMR=10×weight+6.25×height-5×age-161. cm and kg required." },
  { q: "How does BMR affect weight loss?", a: "Your BMR sets the floor for your metabolism. Weight loss calculators use it to find maintenance calories, then subtract a deficit. Lower BMR means lower potential intake." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="BMR Calculator — Find Your Basal Metabolic Rate"
  h1="BMR Calculator"
  description="Free BMR calculator using Mifflin-St Jeor equation. Find calories burned at rest. Includes BMR meaning, calculation, and uses for weight goals."
  path="/guides/bmr-calculator/"
  category="Guides"
  categoryPath="/guides/"
  lead="Your BMR is the calories you burn at rest. Calculate it for weight loss or muscle gain."
  updated="June 2026"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p><strong>BMR calculator</strong> results tell you how many calories your body needs at complete rest. This is the foundation for any nutrition plan.</p>

  <h2>What BMR means for your metabolism</h2>
  <p>BMR accounts for 60–70% of your daily calories burned. The remaining calories come from digesting food (~10%) and movement (~20–30%). Understanding this helps explain why your <a href="/guides/maintenance-calorie-calculator/">maintenance calories</a> matter.</p>

  <h2>BMR calculator formula (Mifflin-St Jeor)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Sex</th><th>BMR Formula</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Men</td><td>BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) - 5 × age + 5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Women</td><td>BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) - 5 × age - 161</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Use our <a href="/bmr-calculator/">BMR calculator</a> for automatic calculation.</p>

  <h2>Average BMR by demographics</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Profile</th><th>Typical BMR range</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Woman: 30, 65kg, 165cm</td><td>~1,300–1,500 calories</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Man: 30, 80kg, 180cm</td><td>~1,700–1,900 calories</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Woman: 50, 75kg</td><td>~1,300–1,500 calories</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Man: 50, 85kg</td><td>~1,600–1,800 calories</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Factors that raise BMR</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Muscle mass:</strong> Each kg of muscle adds 15–25 calories/day</li>
    <li><strong>Youth:</strong> Metabolism slows ~2–5% per decade after 25</li>
    <li><strong>Height:</strong> Larger bodies burn more calories at rest</li>
    <li><strong>Organ size:</strong> Some organs burn more calories than others</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Using BMR for weight loss</h2>
  <p>To lose weight:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Calculate BMR with our calculator</li>
    <li>Multiply by activity factor to get TDEE (<a href="/guides/maintenance-calorie-calculator/">maintenance calories</a>)</li>
    <li>Subtract 250–500 calories for weight loss</li>
    <li>Never go below 1,200 calories (women) or 1,500 (men)</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>BMR vs TDEE vs RMR comparison</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>BMR</td><td>Basal Metabolic Rate</td><td>Calories at complete rest</td></tr>
      <tr><td>TDEE</td><td>Total Daily Energy Expenditure</td><td>Full day's calories burned</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RMR</td><td>Resting Metabolic Rate</td><td>Similar to BMR, easier to measure</td></tr>
  </table>
</ContentLayout>